From 80f1f1fc51508de2f85623c84f3bca296791a206 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunrisepeak <speakshen@163.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 08:30:21 +0900
Subject: [clangd] Prerequisite modules build as a bounded, cancellable DAG

Prerequisite modules are planned once per request: cycles fail before any BMI is built, ready nodes compile in a shared bounded pool, cancellation is observed at compiler boundaries, imports with no buildable unit keep the resolvable modules and the textual preamble, reuse ends with the compile-command generation, and copy-on-read BMIs stay protected by reader leases.

* fix(clangd): cyclic module prerequisites fail before bmi construction

* fix(clangd): third-party imports keep resolvable modules and the preamble

  A direct import whose module unit the project cannot supply (a toolchain
  std seen through a foreign compiler profile, or a registry wrapper with
  no compile command) used to discard every already-built module and return
  FailedPrerequisiteModules forever. Every request then re-ran the
  dependency build and parsed without a preamble, which dominated real
  project completion latency.

  Such imports now stay textual while resolved units keep their BMIs and
  the prerequisite set stays reusable. Files whose imports are all textual
  never load a named-module BMI, so their parse keeps its preamble: the
  PCH+modules mixing upstream guards against cannot arise. The skip verdict
  is cached per on-disk file identity and import-name set and dies with any
  compile-command change, so a module becoming buildable re-derives it
  before a real PCH can be combined with a loaded BMI.

* fix(clangd): prerequisite reuse dies with the compile-command generation

  An empty prerequisite set - every direct import resolved to a textual
  third-party one - stayed reusable forever: canReuse held as long as the
  import-name set was unchanged, so a provider appearing through a
  compile-command change never reached files that had already resolved to
  nothing.

  The builder now counts command-change broadcasts and stamps each
  reusable prerequisite set with that generation; canReuse rejects a
  stale generation before consulting BMI freshness, so the next update
  re-resolves the imports and builds the newly available units. The
  provider-gain scenario in tests/e2e/third_party_import.py drives the
  whole path in one process across the directory CDB's five-second
  revalidation interval.

* fix(clangd): copy-on-read BMIs remain protected by reader leases

  Access time does not prove that a copy is unused and the old collector also removed published BMIs. Each new immutable reader copy carries a unique sidecar protected by a kernel lock for its full shared lifetime. GC only reclaims copies after it acquires their abandoned leases; stable and legacy unleased files remain untouched.

  An in-process ownership set also prevents POSIX fcntl descriptor aliasing from dropping the live process lock. Crash cleanup no longer waits three days or trusts a reused PID.

* perf(modules): ready prerequisites compile in a shared bounded pool

  Independent module units were compiled serially after each direct import traversed its dependency closure. Four header-heavy independent units spent roughly three quarters of a second building prerequisites despite having no dependency ordering between their compilers.

  Plan all buildable roots once on the document thread, reject cycles, and dispatch ready dependency layers in bounded batches. The shared pool defaults to two workers, caps at four physical cores, and keeps scanner/provider state out of compiler workers. Validated prebuilt modules are ready leaves even without provider commands; missing toolchain dependencies remain textual without dropping unrelated buildable roots.

  Each worker receives an immutable BMI snapshot and the request cancellation context. Workers never submit or wait on dependencies, and every dispatched task drains before errors propagate or request state is released. Separate promise futures prevent deferred ThreadPool futures from executing work on the waiting caller and bypassing the global bound. The pool is destroyed before its counters and caches. A focused diamond lit test checks dependency order and semantic completion; raw replays additionally cover failure recovery, prebuilt-only dependencies and simultaneous documents.

* Discard cancelled module builds at compiler boundaries

  A request cancelled after acquiring its source lock or while ExecuteAction compiled a module could still publish the staged BMI and return a reusable prerequisite. Observe cancellation after lock acquisition, main-file reads, compiler preparation and action completion; the existing scope guard removes the uncommitted temporary output.

  Exercise cancellation deterministically through request filesystem reads, separately while fetching the module source and while ExecuteAction reads a header. Both cases must leave no PCM and permit an uncancelled retry using the same builder. ExecuteAction remains synchronous: these checks neither interrupt the action nor enforce a hard runtime or RSS bound.

* Avoid Windows min and max macros in module DAG compilation
